{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["<b>Burn for burn.</b> A burn from fire. And until now it has been speaking of injury in which there is loss of blood, and now [it speaks] of one in which there is no loss of blood but only pain — for example, he burned him with a spit on his nails. They assess: how much would a person in such a condition be willing to take to suffer this pain? (Bava Kamma, chapter 3)." "<b>Wound.</b> It is a blow that draws blood, that opened his flesh — נבר\"ורא בְּלַעַ\"ז. Everything depends on what it is: if there is loss of blood in it, he pays damages; and if he took to bed, he pays lost time, medical costs, embarrassment, and pain. And this verse is superfluous, and in 'וכי יריבון אנשים והכה איש את רעהו' ('when men strive and one strikes another') our Sages derived it to obligate payment for pain even in the place of damage — that even though he pays him the value of his hand, he is not exempt from [payment for] pain. One might say: since the master acquired his hand, he may cut it off however he wishes. Rather, they say: he may cut it off with a medicament, which does not pain him so much — but this one cut it with iron, and caused him pain." "<b>Bruise.</b> It is a blow in which the blood gathers beneath and does not come out, except that the flesh reddens opposite it. And the term חַבּוּרָה is טַק\"א בְּלַעַ\"ז, as in ״וְנָמֵר חֲבַרְבֻּרֹתָיו״ (Jeremiah 13), and its Targum is מַשְׁקוֹפֵי — a term of striking; בַּטְדוּ\"רָא בְּלַעַ\"ז. And so ״שְׁדוּפוֹת קָדִים״ (Genesis 41) — dashed by the east wind, beaten by the wind. And so עַל הַמַּשְׁקוֹף ('on the lintel') — on account of that the door knocks against it."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {2 "Old French la'az left in Hebrew script as written: נבר\"ורא = *navrure* ('wound'); טק\"א = *tache* ('mark/discoloration'); בטדו\"רא = *batudure/bateur* ('beating/blow')."}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:53:47.159875911Z", :translated-from :he, 